Correct Answer: (a) nested structure

Explanation:
A nested structure is a structure that contains another structure as its member.

Example:
struct Date {
    int day;
    int month;
    int year;
};

struct Student {
    char name[50];
    int roll;
    struct Date dob;  // Nested structure — Date inside Student
};

int main() {
    struct Student s1;
    s1.roll = 101;
    s1.dob.day = 15;
    s1.dob.month = 8;
    s1.dob.year = 2003;
    printf("DOB: %d/%d/%d\n", s1.dob.day, s1.dob.month, s1.dob.year);
    // Output: DOB: 15/8/2003
    return 0;
}

Other options:
(b) Self-referential structure: Contains a pointer to the same structure type. Used in linked lists.
    struct Node { int data; struct Node *next; };
(c) Parallel structure: Not a standard C term.
(d) Pointer to structure: A pointer that points to a structure variable.

Answer: (a) nested structure

Correct Answer: (b) at least once

Explanation:
The do-while loop is an exit-controlled loop. The condition is checked AFTER the loop body executes.

Syntax:
do {
    // body
} while (condition);

Since the condition is checked after execution, the body always executes at least once, even if the condition is false from the start.

Example:
int i = 100;
do {
    printf("This executes!\n");  // Executes ONCE
} while (i < 5);  // Condition is false, loop exits

Compare with while loop:
int i = 100;
while (i < 5) {
    printf("This never executes!\n");  // NEVER executes
}

Use Cases for do-while:
1. Menu-driven programs — show menu at least once.
2. Input validation — ask for input at least once.
3. Any scenario where one execution is guaranteed.

Answer: (b) at least once

PART a: Difference between Structure and Union (3 marks)

Structure:
- All members have their OWN separate memory space.
- Total size = sum of sizes of all members.
- All members can hold values simultaneously.
- Keyword: struct

Union:
- All members SHARE the same memory space.
- Total size = size of the LARGEST member.
- Only ONE member can hold a value at a time.
- Keyword: union

Difference Table:
Feature        | Structure              | Union
Keyword        | struct                 | union
Memory         | Separate for each      | Shared (largest member)
Size           | Sum of all members     | Size of largest member
Access         | All members at once    | One member at a time
Modification   | Changing one doesn't   | Changing one overwrites
               | affect others          | others

Example:
struct S { int x; char y; float z; };  // Size = 4+1+4 = 9 (+ padding)
union  U { int x; char y; float z; };  // Size = 4 (largest: float or int)

---

PART b: Student Data Program (7 marks)

#include <stdio.h>

struct Student {
    int roll;
    char name[50];
    float marks[3];  // 3 subjects
};

int main() {
    int n, i, j;
    float avg;

    printf("Enter number of students: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    struct Student s[n];  // Array of structures

    // Input data for each student
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("\n--- Student %d ---\n", i + 1);
        printf("Enter Roll No: ");
        scanf("%d", &s[i].roll);
        printf("Enter Name: ");
        scanf("%s", s[i].name);
